<section>
<title>Curved Spacetime</title>


<introduction>
<p>
Now we repeat our experiment with the two circles, but let's go into
space, make the circles large, and center them around a spherical
object of mass <m>M</m>. This mass could be a planet or a star, or maybe a
black hole. In this case we are not on the curved surface of a
sphere, rather we draw our circles on a horizontal plane. But,
surprise! We would again find that <m>\Delta s</m>, the radial distance
traveled, is not equal to <m>\Delta r</m>.
</p>

<p>
So <em>now</em> what's going on? In general relativity, mass distorts
space and time, and what we are measuring is the distortion of
space.<fn>And just like being on a curved surface, we would find
  that our distorted horizontal plane breaks other rules of planar
  geometry, such as the angles of a triangle not adding to
  <m>180^\circ</m>.</fn> A curved surface is a helpful way to visualize
distorted space, and for that reason, physicists refer to the
distorted spacetime predicted by general relativity as <em>curved
spacetime</em>. But it's important to remember that the curved surface
is only a visualization tool.
</p>

<p>
So what does general relativity tell us about distorted space and
distorted time (which, from now on we will refer to as curved
spacetime). How is it that <q>mass tells spacetime how to curve</q>?
Generally, the answer requires one to solve a set of nasty coupled
nonlinear differential equations known as Einstein's equations, which
is well beyond the scope of this course. Fortunately, for the case of
the spacetime outside a spherical mass, there is a relatively simple
solution, which was found by Karl
Schwarzschild.<fn>Schwarzschild found this solution to
  Einstein's equations in 1915, the same year that Einstein published
  his general relativity.  Schwarzschild then died a year later due to a disease he had caught while serving in
  World War I.</fn> We now describe his solution.
</p>
</introduction>


<subsection>
<title>Curved Space</title>
<p>
Consider an equatorial plane, that is, a two-dimensional <m>x</m>-<m>y</m> plane
of space with spherical mass <m>M</m> centered at the origin. Define the
coordinate <m>r</m> in terms of the circumference <m>C</m> of a circle centered
about this mass; that is, take a string of length <m>C</m> and make it into
a circle centered about the mass. Then define <m>r=C/(2\pi)</m>.
</p>

<p>
Now, moving radially outward a small distance <m>\Delta s</m> will increase
the coordinate <m>r</m> by an amount <m>\Delta r</m>. If space were not distorted,
we would expect <m>\Delta s = \Delta r</m>. But since the mass <m>M</m>
distorts space, <m>\Delta s</m> is not equal to <m>\Delta r</m>, but rather they
are related by
<men xml:id="eq_Schwarzschild_ds" >
  \Delta s = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - R_s/r}} \Delta r.
</men>
</p>

<p>
Here <m>R_s</m> is the Schwarzschild radius, defined as
<men xml:id="eq_Schwarzschild_radius" >
  R_s = \frac{2 G M}{c^2},
</men>
where <m>G</m> is Newton's gravitational constant and <m>c</m> is the speed of
light. Please note: the Schwarzschild radius <m>R_s</m> is not the radius
of the spherical mass <m>M</m>. It is simply a length that appears in
( <xref ref="eq_Schwarzschild_ds"></xref> to tell us how distorted space is. This
solution of Einstein's equations applies for the region outside of the
mass <m>M</m> and for <m>r>R_s</m>.
</p>

<p>
We can visualize this distorted space by introducing a fictional
third dimension and curving our <m>x</m>-<m>y</m> plane into it, as shown in
<xref ref="fig_curved-space">Figure</xref>. Like our previous example with the
surface of the sphere, this curved surface
illustrates why <m>\Delta s > \Delta r</m>
and accurately captures the distortion of the <m>x</m>-<m>y</m> plane.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig_curved-space" >
<caption>Curved space near a black hole.  The distance <m>\Delta s</m> measured
radially along the surface between circles of radial coordinate  <m>r_1</m> 
and <m>r_2</m> is larger than their  difference <m>\Delta r</m>.</caption>
<image width="75%" source="black-hole.png" />
</figure>
<p>
But don't confuse this fictional third dimension with the physical
<m>z</m> direction. The curvature is only describing the distortion of
the horizontal <m>x</m>-<m>y</m> plane.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex_curved_space">
<statement>
<p>
How curved is space near the sun? That is, how different are
<m>\Delta s</m> and <m>\Delta r</m>?
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution: </em> First, find the Schwarzschild radius from
<xref ref="eq_Schwarzschild_radius" /> with <m>M=M_\text{sun} = 1.99\times
10^{30}\,\text{kg}</m>:
<men>
  R_s = \frac{2GM}{c^2} = \frac{2\times 6.67\times 10^{-11}\,\frac{\text{N\(\cdot\)m\(^2\)} }{\text{kg\(^2\)} }\times 1.99\times 10^{30}\,\text{kg} }{(3.00\times 10^8\,\text{m/s} )^2} = 2950\,\text{m}
</men>
</p>

<p>
Then use the general relativity relation between <m>\Delta s</m> and <m>\Delta r</m>,
<xref ref="eq_Schwarzschild_ds" />, at the location
<m>r=R_\text{sun} =6.96\times 10^{8}\,\text{m}</m> to get
<md>
  <mrow>  \Delta s \amp = \biggl(1-\frac{2950\,\text{m} }{6.96\times 10^{8}\,\text{m} }\biggr)^{-1/2}\Delta r</mrow>
  <mrow>  \amp = 1.0000021\,\Delta r.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
That's not looking very curved, but it turns out to be enough to direct the
motion of all the planets in their orbits about the sun!
</p>
</statement>
</example>
</subsection>


<subsection>
<title>Clock Rates</title>
<p>
The spherical mass <m>M</m> distorts both space <em>and</em> time, so now
let's turn to the time component. The Schwarzschild solution states
that a clock located at coordinate <m>r</m> runs slower than a clock
located very far from the mass. Consider a pair of events and let
<m>\Delta t_r</m> represent the time between these events as measured by a
stationary clock at coordinate <m>r</m>. Further, let <m>\Delta t_\infty</m> be
the time between the same pair of events as measured by a stationary
clock far away. These time increments are related by
<men xml:id="eq_Schwarzschild_dt" >
  \Delta t_r = \sqrt{1-\frac{R_s}{r}}\;\Delta t_\infty.
</men>
</p>

<p>
Note that <m>\Delta t_r\lt \Delta t_\infty</m> for any finite <m>r</m>. This means
that clocks closer to the mass <m>M</m> will run slower than clocks farther
away, as illustrated in <xref ref="fig_clocks">Figure</xref>. A nice mnemonic is
<q>Low clocks are slow clocks</q>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig_clocks" >
<caption>The clock closer to the mass <m>M</m> runs slower than the clock
  far away.  Low clocks are slow clocks!</caption>
<image width="56%" source="clocks.png" />
</figure>
<p>
This is a completely separate effect from the proper time relation that
we studied in special relativity. There the clocks were moving relative
to one another, while here both clocks are stationary with respect to the
mass <m>M</m> and have no relative motion. Yet general relativity predicts
that the clocks still run at different rates.
</p>

<example>
<title>Clocks on the Earth's Surface</title>
<statement>
<p>
How much slower does a clock at sea level run than one far away from
Earth?
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution: </em>
</p>

<p>
First, find the Schwarzschild radius for the Earth, with <m>M=5.97\times
10^{24}\,\text{kg}</m>:
<md>
  <mrow>  R_s \amp = \frac{2GM}{c^2} = \frac{2\times 6.67\times 10^{-11}\,\frac{\text{N\(\cdot\)m\(^2\)} }{\text{kg\(^2\)} } \times
    5.97\times 10^{24}\,\text{kg} }{(3.00\times 10^8\,\text{m/s} )^2}
  = 0.00885\,\text{m}</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
That's a lot smaller than the radius of the Earth!
Next use <xref ref="eq_Schwarzschild_dt" /> with <m>r=R_E = 6.37\times
10^{6}\,\text{m}</m>
<md>
  <mrow>  \Delta t_{R_E} \amp =
  \sqrt{1-\frac{0.00885\,\text{m} }{6.37\times 10^{6}\,\text{m} }}\;\Delta t_\infty
   \approx (1 - 6.95\times 10^{-10}) \Delta t_\infty.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
A useful way to express this result is the rate at which a clock on Earth
falls behind:
<md>
  <mrow>  \Delta t_\infty - \Delta t_{R_E} \amp = \Delta t_\infty -
     (1 - 6.95\times 10^{-10}) \Delta t_\infty</mrow>
  <mrow>  \amp = 6.95\times 10^{-10} \Delta t_\infty.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
The clock on Earth falls behind the far away clock an amount
<m>6.95\times 10^{-10}\,\text{s}</m> every second. Thus, for the clock on
Earth to get behind the remote clock by one second, it takes
<men>
  \frac{1}{6.95\times 10^{-10}} \text{seconds}  = 1.44\times
10^9\,\text{s}    \approx 46\,\text{yr} .
</men>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<p>
We don't notice these time effects much near Earth, but these small
differences are essential to be accounted for in the design and
operations of GPS devices. For a more dramatic example, see Problem
4.
</p>
</subsection>

</section>
